};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "carnivor protein powder",
      "musclemeds carnivor protein powder",
      "carnivore protein powder",
      "carnivor beef protein isolate",
      "is carnivor third party tested"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Beef Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-beef-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Carnivor Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      MuscleMeds Carnivor checked by its label: what is in the beef isolate, whether it is third-party tested, what it costs per 25 g, and why it is not a carnivore-diet powder.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#what-is-carnivor">What Is Carnivor?</a>
      <a href="#ingredients">What Is in It?</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#carnivore-diet">Carnivore-Diet Friendly?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#taste-mixing">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#carnivor-vs">Carnivor vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          MuscleMeds Carnivor is a cheap, Informed Choice certified (per the brand) beef protein isolate with 23 g of protein per scoop and about $1.11 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw. What changes the verdict is the label: added creatine, maltodextrin and artificial sweeteners. Below we cover the ingredients, testing, cost, buyer feedback and better picks for carnivore dieters.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Carnivor Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            MuscleMeds Carnivor is a cheap, widely sold beef protein isolate with 23 g of protein, 0 g of fat and 0 g of sugar per scoop, per the label at the time of writing, and MuscleMeds says it is Informed Choice certified. At the Amazon price we saw on November 21, 2026, it costs about $1.11 per 25 g of protein, which is roughly half what the grass-fed beef isolates charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is the label. Carnivor is not a single-ingredient beef powder: the Chocolate tub lists creatine, added BCAAs, maltodextrin, artificial flavors, sucralose, acesulfame potassium, xanthan gum and an antifoam blend containing soybean oil. That makes it a dairy-free gym shake, not a carnivore-diet food, whatever the name suggests.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Carnivor by the Numbers (Chocolate label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">23 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 36.4 g scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fat and sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.11</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g at the Amazon price seen</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a dairy-free, lactose-free protein shake at whey-like prices, you like dessert and cereal flavors, and sucralose does not bother you. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you follow a strict carnivore or animal-based diet, avoid artificial sweeteners, or want a short label you can read in five seconds. For those buyers the unflavored isolates in our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">best beef protein powder guide</a> are the better fit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Carnivor and the Beef Isolates Worth Comparing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carnivor first, then three Amazon alternatives: a one-ingredient isolate for carnivore dieters, the best-known grass-fed option, and the most protein per scoop. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 21, 2026.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-carnivor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is MuscleMeds Carnivor Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carnivor is a hydrolyzed beef protein isolate made by MuscleMeds, a sports-nutrition brand that markets it as the world's first beef protein isolate and the best-selling beef protein brand. Those are the brand's own claims, and we could not check the sales ranking independently. What we can say is that Carnivor is everywhere: Amazon, Vitacost, Walmart and most supplement shops carry it, and the 56-serving Chocolate tub on Amazon showed more than 4,400 ratings when we looked.
          </p>

          <p>
            People search the name three ways. "Carnivor protein powder" and "MuscleMeds Carnivor protein powder" are the brand searches, and this page answers both. The third, "carnivore protein powder" with an e, is usually someone on the carnivore diet looking for an all-animal protein. The two get mixed up constantly, and the difference matters, because Carnivor's label includes plant-derived and synthetic ingredients that a carnivore dieter would not choose. We cover that in its own section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            The product line has also grown beyond the original isolate. MuscleMeds sells Carnivor Mass, a high-carbohydrate weight gainer, and Carnivor Shred, a version with added fat-burning ingredients. This review covers the standard Carnivor Beef Protein Isolate, the tub most people mean when they search the name. If you were looking for a gainer instead, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">guide to protein powder for weight gain</a> and <a href="/mass-gainer-vs-protein-powder/">mass gainer vs protein powder</a> are the better starting points.
          </p>

          <p>
            Why beef at all? Beef protein isolate exists mainly for people who cannot or will not use dairy. It has no lactose and no milk proteins, so it suits people with a milk allergy or real lactose intolerance who still want an animal protein. MuscleMeds also leans on a clinical study it says showed lean-mass and strength gains with Carnivor, but that is marketing from the brand, and a single sponsored study is not a reason to pick one protein over another. For most lifters, total daily protein matters far more than the source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Product names vary between retailers. Some listings say Hydrolyzed Beef Protein Isolate, others say Bioengineered Beef Protein Isolate, and a Vitacost listing for Vanilla Caramel notes that the product contains bioengineered food ingredients. That disclosure is a federal labeling term for ingredients from genetically engineered sources, and it most likely relates to ingredients such as the soybean oil or maltodextrin rather than the beef. MuscleMeds does not say which ingredient triggers it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Carnivor Beef Protein Isolate?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per the Chocolate supplement-facts panel at the time of writing, one 36.4 g scoop of Carnivor gives 120 calories, 23 g of protein, 0 g of fat, 0 mg of cholesterol, 7 g of carbohydrate including 2 g of fiber, 0 g of sugar, 370 mg of sodium, 230 mg of potassium and 1.6 mg of iron. Numbers move a little by flavor: a Vitacost listing for Vanilla Caramel shows a 31.7 g scoop with the same 23 g of protein and 120 calories, 6 g of carbohydrate and 340 mg of sodium.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per scoop (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chocolate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Vanilla Caramel</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scoop size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36.4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31.7 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">23 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate / sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 g / 0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 g / 0 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">370 mg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">340 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Servings per tub</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">56</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">56</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The ingredient list is longer than most buyers expect. On the Chocolate label, the first ingredient is CARNIVOR-BPI, a blend of hydrolyzed beef protein isolate, creatine monohydrate, BCAAs (L-leucine, L-valine, L-isoleucine) and what MuscleMeds calls Anabolic Nitrogen Retention Technology intermediates: glutamine-alpha-ketoglutarate, ornithine-alpha-ketoglutarate, alpha-ketoglutarate and alpha-ketoisocaproate. After the blend come maltodextrin, cocoa, natural and artificial flavors, salt, silica, acesulfame potassium, xanthan gum, sunflower lecithin, sucralose and a multi-part antifoam.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two details deserve attention. First, the blend has no individual amounts, so you cannot tell how much creatine or added leucine each scoop holds. Added free amino acids can also count toward a label's protein figure, which means part of the 23 g may come from added BCAAs rather than the beef itself. That is common in sports proteins and not a reason to panic, but it is a reason not to treat 23 g as 23 g of intact beef protein. If you want a measured creatine dose, take it separately; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">protein powder with creatine guide</a> covers products that print the amount.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the antifoam. Beef isolate foams heavily when shaken, and the label's directions even warn that the protein may foam. To control it, MuscleMeds adds an antifoam containing propylene glycol esters, polyglycerol oleate, refined soybean oil, dimethylpolysiloxane, hydrophobic silica, polysorbate 60, sorbitan monostearate and less than 2 percent paraffin. These are approved food additives used in small amounts, but they explain why some buyers call the label overprocessed.
          </p>

          <p>
            On sweeteners, Carnivor uses two artificial ones, acesulfame potassium and sucralose, in the flavors we checked. There is no sugar on the panel. The jar says it is free of fat, lactose, cholesterol, sugar and gluten, contains no gelatin and is made with USDA-inspected beef, and Amazon's title says it is Halal certified. Those statements come from MuscleMeds and its listings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Carnivor Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, according to MuscleMeds: Carnivor is Informed Choice certified, tested for over 200 substances on the World Anti-Doping Agency list, and made in a GMP-compliant facility. The Informed Choice logo is printed on the Chocolate label, and MuscleMeds appears on Informed Choice's public list of certified brands. We could not open the product-level search results on the Informed Choice registry when we checked on November 21, 2026, so the certification of this exact product and flavor is the brand's claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            It helps to know what Informed Choice covers. The program screens samples for banned substances, which matters to drug-tested athletes worried about contamination with stimulants or anabolic agents. It is not a test of protein content, taste or heavy metals, and it does not mean the product is healthier than an uncertified one. If you compete under a drug-testing program, search the exact product, flavor and lot on the Informed Choice website before you buy, because certification can vary by batch.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">HOW TO CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Search the registry, not the logo</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Go to the Informed Choice supplement search, look up MuscleMeds Carnivor, and match the flavor and batch number on your tub. A logo on a label is a start; a registry listing is the proof. Drug-tested athletes who want NSF Certified for Sport instead can look at Designs for Sport beef protein.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Heavy metals are a separate question. The Amazon listing for Carnivor carries a California Proposition 65 warning that the product can expose you to chemicals including lead. Prop 65 warnings are common on protein powders, cocoa products and many foods, and they use very low thresholds, so the warning alone does not tell you how much lead is in a scoop. MuscleMeds does not publish lot-level heavy-metal results for the isolate that we could find.
          </p>

          <p>
            For context, Consumer Reports' October 2025 protein testing included Carnivor Mass, the gainer, not the standard isolate reviewed here. As covered in our <a href="/best-beef-protein-powder/">beef protein guide</a>, the gainer came in at about 247 percent of Consumer Reports' deliberately cautious daily level of concern for lead per serving, partly because gainer servings are huge. We found no Consumer Reports result for Carnivor isolate. If lead worries you, ask MuscleMeds for a recent certificate of analysis and read our explainer on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are a drug-tested athlete who wants a beef protein with a certification you can verify in seconds, <a href="/designs-for-sport-beef-protein-review/">Designs for Sport beef protein</a> is listed as NSF Certified for Sport in NSF's database. It costs nearly three times as much per gram, so it is only worth it if the certification is the point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carnivore-diet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Carnivor Protein Powder Carnivore-Diet Friendly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No, not for a strict carnivore diet. Carnivor's name refers to beef, not the diet, and its label includes maltodextrin, cocoa, artificial flavors, xanthan gum, sucralose, acesulfame potassium, sunflower lecithin and an antifoam made partly from soybean oil. A carnivore diet, as most followers practice it, excludes plant foods, sweeteners and seed oils, so Carnivor breaks several of its rules at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the most common mix-up around the product. Many people type "carnivore protein powder" with an e because they eat only animal foods and want a shake that fits. Search engines then show Carnivor, because the words look almost the same. The result is a buyer who expected a pure meat powder and opened a tub of Fruity Cereal beef protein sweetened with sucralose. Our guide to <a href="/best-paleo-protein-powder/">paleo protein powder</a> covers the same issue for paleo eaters.
          </p>

          <p>
            How strict you need to be is your call. Some people following a relaxed animal-based diet are fine with a little sweetener in a protein shake, and Carnivor's 0 g of sugar and 0 g of fat fit some of their goals. But the maltodextrin and fiber still add 6 to 7 g of carbohydrate per scoop, per the labels we checked, which is more than a carnivore or strict keto eater usually wants from a shake.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to buy instead for a carnivore diet</h3>

          <p>
            Look for an unflavored beef isolate with a one-ingredient label. <a href="/be-well-by-kelly-protein-powder-review/">Be Well by Kelly Unflavored</a> lists only grass-fed beef protein isolate, with 23 g of protein and 0 g of carbohydrate per scoop, per the brand. Equip Prime Unflavored is another single-ingredient option, per Equip. Both cost more per gram than Carnivor, but you get exactly what a carnivore dieter is looking for and nothing else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bone broth protein is the other route. It is all-animal by nature, but it is mostly collagen, which is low in leucine, the amino acid most tied to muscle building. If you go that way, read our <a href="/ancient-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Ancient Nutrition protein powder review</a> and our <a href="/best-bone-broth-protein-powder/">best bone broth protein guide</a> first, and plan to use a larger serving.
          </p>
        </div>

