        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Carnivor, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carnivor makes sense for lifters who need a dairy-free protein and care most about price. If whey gives you bloating, you are allergic to milk, or you simply want an animal protein without lactose, Carnivor gets you 23 g of protein per scoop for about $1.11 per 25 g at the price we saw. Our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">dairy-free protein powder guide</a> list other options in the same lane.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also suits athletes in drug-tested sport who want an inexpensive certified option, provided they confirm the Informed Choice listing for their batch. And it suits people who like sweet, playful flavors; few beef proteins offer anything like Fruity Cereal or Rocket Pop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you follow a strict carnivore, animal-based or clean-label diet. The sucralose, acesulfame potassium, maltodextrin, artificial flavors and antifoam are exactly what those buyers try to avoid. Skip it too if you want to know your creatine dose, since the label hides it inside a blend, or if you are sensitive to sucralose; our list of <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a> is the place to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            A health note: Carnivor includes creatine and added amino acids, and its label warns it is not for children or pregnant or nursing women. If you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take prescription medication, or have a food allergy, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. The label also lists soybean oil inside its antifoam, which matters to anyone told to avoid soy.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure beef protein is right for you at all, compare it with whey first. A good whey isolate is often cheaper per gram, higher in leucine and easier to mix. Beef isolate earns its place only when dairy is off the table, and Carnivor is the cheapest way to fill that slot.
          </p>
        </div>

