              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                MuscleMeds prints the Informed Choice logo and says Carnivor is certified, but drug-tested athletes should still search the exact flavor and batch on the Informed Choice website. Certification works by batch, and a quick registry check is cheap insurance against a failed test and a ruined season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting all 23 g as beef protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The protein blend includes added BCAAs, creatine and keto-acid intermediates without individual amounts. Added amino acids can count toward the label's protein figure. Treat Carnivor's 23 g as a ceiling for intact beef protein, and use a slightly larger serving if it is your main protein at a meal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small tub to save money</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 28-serving tub looks cheaper at checkout, but at the Amazon prices we saw it cost about $1.54 per 25 g against $1.11 for the 56-serving tub. Once you know you like the taste, the big tub saves roughly 40 percent per gram of protein.
              </p>
